<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ban Chao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">32–102</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ban Chao was a general of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> who brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> back under Chinese control. From the 70s CE he used Shule (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>) as a base, subduing kingdoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Karasahr</a> in 94, and served as Protector General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a> from 91. In 97 he sent his envoy Gan Ying westwards towards the Roman world. He was the brother of the historians Ban Gu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-zhao">Ban Zhao</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ban-chao.jpg" alt="Ban Chao" width="320" height="683" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B1%89%E7%8F%AD%E8%B6%85%E7%AB%8B%E5%83%8F.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ban Chao was born in Fufeng, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, the son of the historian Ban Biao and the younger brother of Ban Gu and the older brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-zhao">Ban Zhao</a>, who wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a>. His family was poor, and he worked as a copy-clerk for the government. Emperor Ming thought highly of him and made him a clerk in the orchid terrace, but he was too ambitious for the post and was dismissed. He is said to have been strong-willed as a young man, with complete disregard for formal conduct. When Ban Gu was appointed to the imperial library, Ban Chao and his mother moved to the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 73 General Dou Gu attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, and Ban Chao was appointed assistant major. He was sent with Guo Xun on an embassy to the western regions. At the capital of Shanshan, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> also received envoys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a>; Ban Chao and his small party killed them and sent their heads to the king, who sent hostages to the Han. Ban Chao expelled the Xiongnu from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and brought its peoples under Chinese rule, generally outnumbered but skilled at playing on the divisions among his opponents. Khotan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> came under Chinese rule by 74. He was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and sent out again four years later, in the reign of Emperor Zhang.</p><p>In 84 he obtained the military help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> against the Kangju. In 90 the Kushans, who had been refused a Han princess, marched on him with 70,000 men and were defeated by his smaller force. In 91 he was awarded the title of Protector General and stationed at Kucha, and in 94 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Karashahr</a>; more than fifty kingdoms then presented hostages. In 97 he sent his envoy Gan Ying, who reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and left the first recorded Chinese account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 102 Ban Chao retired as Protector General because of age and ill health, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> at the age of 70. He died there the following month. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> power in the Western Territories increased again, and later Chinese emperors did not reach so far west until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. His youngest son Ban Yong joined his campaigns and kept a central military role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> into the 120s. The saying Throw away your writing brush and join the military is based on words attributed to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ban Chao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Kushan king who had been refused a Han princess sent his viceroy Xie with 70,000 men across the Pamirs in 90 CE against the Han general <b>Ban Chao</b>, who cut off their supplies and forced them to withdraw, after which the Kushans sent tribute to the Han court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kucha later allied with the Xiongnu and the Yuezhi against the general <b>Ban Chao</b>, and formally submitted in 124 CE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Han general <b>Ban Chao</b> subdued it in 94, and the Tang Dynasty conquered it in 644 and made it one of the Four Garrisons of Anxi.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-karasahr">Kingdom of Karasahr</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 70s CE the Han general <b>Ban Chao</b> used it as his base for reconquering the Western Regions.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shule">Kingdom of Shule</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Qindu District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.34,108.71&amp;year=77" aria-label="Qindu District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ban Chao"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4208 4590h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4208 4590h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.34,108.71&amp;year=77">What was here in 77 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
